A **stack** is last-in-first-out; a **queue** is first-in-first-out. Both matter because they let you remember things in the exact order a problem needs them, without scanning.

To put it simply: a stack is for "match this against the most recent unmatched thing," and a queue is for "process things in the order they arrived."

## The Problem — why a stack exists

Validate `"{[()]}"`. Brackets nest, so you can't just count them — `"([)]"` has equal counts of everything and is still invalid. What you actually need to know at each closing bracket is: **what was the most recent unclosed opening bracket?**

Scanning backwards from every closer to find its partner is O(n²), and gets tangled fast. But "most recent unclosed thing" is precisely what a stack gives you in O(1).

## The Solution

Push every opener. On a closer, pop and check the pair matches. If the stack is empty when you need to pop, or non-empty at the end, it's invalid.

```javascript
function isValid(s) {
  const pairs = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };
  const stack = [];

  for (const char of s) {
    if (char === '(' || char === '[' || char === '{') {
      stack.push(char);
    } else {
      // Closer with nothing open, or the wrong opener on top
      if (stack.pop() !== pairs[char]) return false;
    }
  }

  return stack.length === 0;   // anything left open is invalid
}
```

**O(n) time, O(n) space.** The `stack.length === 0` check at the end is the part people forget — without it `"((("` passes.

## What actually is a stack in JavaScript?

**It's just an array.** `push` and `pop` operate on the end, both O(1). There is no `Stack` class and you don't need one.

A queue is also just an array — but with a catch. `shift()` removes from the *front*, which means re-indexing every remaining element: **O(n)**. In a loop that makes your O(n) BFS quietly O(n²).

For interview-sized inputs `shift()` is fine, and you should say so rather than over-engineering. But **mention that you know**, because that awareness is the signal:

```javascript
// Fine for interviews, but O(n) per shift
const queue = [start];
while (queue.length) {
  const node = queue.shift();
}

// O(1) alternative: move a read pointer instead of mutating
const queue = [start];
let head = 0;
while (head < queue.length) {
  const node = queue[head++];
}
```

## The monotonic stack — the one that separates candidates

Question shape: **"next greater element"**, **"days until a warmer temperature"**, **"largest rectangle in a histogram."**

Keep a stack whose values are always in sorted order. When a new element violates that order, pop everything it beats — and each pop *is an answer*.

```javascript
function nextGreater(nums) {
  const result = new Array(nums.length).fill(-1);
  const stack = [];   // holds indices, values decreasing

  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
    // Every index this new value beats gets its answer now
    while (stack.length && nums[stack[stack.length - 1]] < nums[i]) {
      result[stack.pop()] = nums[i];
    }
    stack.push(i);
  }

  return result;
}
```

Still **O(n)** — same argument as the sliding window: every index is pushed once and popped at most once.

## When to use a queue

Anything **level by level** or **shortest path in an unweighted graph**. BFS explores everything one step away, then everything two steps away — so the first time it reaches a target, that path is the shortest. DFS gives no such guarantee.

```javascript
function levelOrder(root) {
  if (!root) return [];
  const result = [];
  const queue = [root];

  while (queue.length) {
    const levelSize = queue.length;     // snapshot before adding children
    const level = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
      const node = queue.shift();
      level.push(node.val);
      if (node.left) queue.push(node.left);
      if (node.right) queue.push(node.right);
    }

    result.push(level);
  }

  return result;
}
```

Capturing `levelSize` *before* the inner loop is the whole trick — it's what separates one level from the next.

## Trigger phrases

**Stack:** "matching", "nesting", "balanced", "undo", "most recent", "next greater", "evaluate an expression", "backtrack".

**Queue:** "level by level", "shortest path", "BFS", "in order of arrival", "process in batches".

## Trade-offs and follow-ups

**"Why not recursion instead of a stack?"** — Recursion *is* a stack, the call stack. An explicit stack is what you use when the input might be deep enough to overflow it (JS blows up somewhere around 10k frames), or when you need to pause mid-traversal.

**"BFS or DFS here?"** — BFS for shortest path and level-order, but it holds an entire level in memory — on a wide tree that's expensive. DFS uses O(height) space but gives no shortest-path guarantee. Naming this trade-off is the answer they want.

