A **linked list** is a chain of objects where each one holds a value and a pointer to the next. There is no indexing — to reach the 5th node you must walk through the first four.

To put it simply: it's an array that traded random access for cheap insertion.

## The Problem it solves

Inserting into the middle of an array means shifting every element after it: **O(n)**. In a linked list, insertion is rewriting two pointers: **O(1)** — *if you're already holding the node*.

The trade is that arrays give you `arr[i]` in O(1) and linked lists give you O(n) traversal. Which is why frontend work uses arrays for almost everything, and linked lists show up mainly inside other structures — an **LRU cache** is the one you're most likely to actually meet ([/coding/28](/coding/28)).

## What actually is a linked list?

**It's just an object with a `next` property.** No class required:

```javascript
const node = { val: 1, next: { val: 2, next: { val: 3, next: null } } };
```

That's a complete linked list. Everything else — reversal, cycle detection, merging — is pointer rearrangement on plain objects. Realizing there's no magic here removes most of the intimidation.

## Pattern 1 — Reversal

The one you're most likely to be asked. Walk the list, flipping each `next` to point backwards, carrying three pointers.

```javascript
function reverseList(head) {
  let prev = null;
  let current = head;

  while (current) {
    const next = current.next;   // save it BEFORE we destroy the link
    current.next = prev;         // flip
    prev = current;              // advance
    current = next;
  }

  return prev;                   // prev is the new head
}
```

**Saving `next` first is the entire problem.** The moment you write `current.next = prev`, the rest of the list is unreachable. Say that out loud while typing — it's exactly the detail being tested.

Trace it on `1 → 2 → 3` on paper before the interview. Three iterations. If you can produce that trace under pressure, you can write this cold.

## Pattern 2 — Fast and slow pointers

Two pointers moving at different speeds. Slow moves one step, fast moves two.

**Find the middle:** when fast reaches the end, slow is halfway.

```javascript
function middleNode(head) {
  let slow = head;
  let fast = head;

  while (fast && fast.next) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
  }

  return slow;
}
```

**Detect a cycle (Floyd's algorithm):** if there's a loop, fast eventually laps slow and they land on the same node. If there isn't, fast hits `null`.

```javascript
function hasCycle(head) {
  let slow = head;
  let fast = head;

  while (fast && fast.next) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) return true;
  }

  return false;
}
```

Why they must meet: inside a cycle, fast closes the gap on slow by exactly one node per step. A gap that shrinks by one each step reaches zero. It cannot step over slow. That explanation is the follow-up — have it ready.

**O(n) time, O(1) space.** The alternative — a `Set` of visited nodes — is also O(n) time but O(n) space. Naming both is the trade-off answer.

## Pattern 3 — The dummy head

Whenever the head itself might change, allocate a fake node in front. It removes every "is this the first one?" special case.

```javascript
function mergeTwoLists(a, b) {
  const dummy = { val: 0, next: null };
  let tail = dummy;

  while (a && b) {
    if (a.val <= b.val) { tail.next = a; a = a.next; }
    else                { tail.next = b; b = b.next; }
    tail = tail.next;
  }

  tail.next = a ?? b;    // attach whatever remains
  return dummy.next;     // skip the dummy
}
```

Without the dummy you'd need a branch for "is the result list empty yet?" on every iteration. Reaching for it unprompted reads as someone who has actually done this.

## Trigger phrases

**"reverse the list"**, **"detect a cycle"**, **"find the middle"**, **"merge sorted lists"**, **"remove the Nth from the end"**, **"O(1) space"** on a list problem, **"LRU cache"**.

## Trade-offs and follow-ups

**"Reverse it recursively."** — Doable, and it's O(n) stack space instead of O(1). Say that trade-off; the iterative version is usually the better answer.

**"Remove the Nth node from the end in one pass."** — Advance a fast pointer `n` steps first, then move both until fast hits the end. Slow lands just before the target. Combine with a dummy head so removing the first node needs no special case.

**"Why does frontend care?"** — Honestly: mostly it doesn't, directly. But an LRU cache is a linked list plus a hash map, and that's a genuinely useful frontend structure. React's own hook state is a linked list per fiber, which is *why* hooks must be called in the same order every render ([/react-guide/12](/react-guide/12)). That connection is a great thing to mention.

Practice: [/coding/44](/coding/44) (Reverse Linked List).

A **tree** is a set of nodes where each has children and exactly one parent, with a single root and no cycles. A **binary tree** allows at most two children per node.

To put it simply: it's a linked list that can branch — and almost everything you do with one is a recursive walk.

## Why frontend engineers actually care

This isn't abstract. The **DOM is a tree**. React's **fiber tree** is a tree. Your **component hierarchy**, your **route config**, any **nested comment thread**, any **file explorer** — trees. When an interviewer asks you to traverse a binary tree, they're checking whether you can reason about the structure that every UI is built from.

## What actually is a tree node?

**A plain object.** Same as a linked list, with two pointers instead of one:

```javascript
const node = { val: 1, left: null, right: null };
```

And the traversal is a function that calls itself on `left` and `right`. That's the entire subject. The reason recursion fits so naturally is that **a tree is defined recursively** — a tree is a node whose children are trees. Your code mirrors the shape of the data.

## DFS — go deep first

Three orders, differing only in *when you touch the node* relative to its children:

```javascript
function inorder(node, out = []) {
  if (!node) return out;        // base case — the empty tree
  inorder(node.left, out);
  out.push(node.val);           // ← visit between the subtrees
  inorder(node.right, out);
  return out;
}
```

- **Preorder** (`visit, left, right`) — copying or serializing a tree; you need the parent before its children.
- **Inorder** (`left, visit, right`) — on a **binary search tree this yields sorted order.** That's the fact they're testing.
- **Postorder** (`left, right, visit`) — when a node's answer depends on its children: computing heights, deleting a tree, evaluating an expression.

The `if (!node) return` base case is what makes recursion on trees so clean — you never check "does this child exist" before recursing, you just let the call handle `null`.

**O(n) time** (every node once), **O(h) space** for the call stack, where `h` is the height. On a balanced tree that's O(log n); on a degenerate one-sided tree it's O(n).

## BFS — go wide first

Level by level, using a queue ([/algorithm/6](/algorithm/6)):

```javascript
function levelOrder(root) {
  if (!root) return [];
  const result = [];
  const queue = [root];

  while (queue.length) {
    const levelSize = queue.length;    // snapshot BEFORE pushing children
    const level = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < levelSize; i++) {
      const node = queue.shift();
      level.push(node.val);
      if (node.left) queue.push(node.left);
      if (node.right) queue.push(node.right);
    }

    result.push(level);
  }

  return result;
}
```

Snapshotting `levelSize` before the inner loop is what groups the output by level. Without it, the queue grows while you're draining it and the levels smear together.

## Bottom-up: returning values up the tree

The pattern that handles most "compute something about the tree" questions. Solve the children, then combine:

```javascript
function maxDepth(node) {
  if (!node) return 0;
  return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(node.left), maxDepth(node.right));
}

function isBalanced(node) {
  function height(n) {
    if (!n) return 0;
    const left = height(n.left);
    if (left === -1) return -1;              // propagate failure upward
    const right = height(n.right);
    if (right === -1) return -1;
    if (Math.abs(left - right) > 1) return -1;
    return 1 + Math.max(left, right);
  }
  return height(node) !== -1;
}
```

Using a sentinel (`-1`) to carry "already failed" up the recursion avoids re-walking subtrees, keeping it O(n) instead of O(n²). Worth explaining if you use it.

You already have this framing in [/algorithm/1](/algorithm/1) — *top-down to reach the leaves, bottom-up to build the answer.* It's the same idea, and it generalizes past trees to any nested JSON.

## Binary Search Tree

A binary tree with an ordering invariant: **everything in the left subtree is smaller, everything right is larger.** That makes search O(h) — binary search, but on pointers.

```javascript
function searchBST(root, target) {
  let node = root;
  while (node) {
    if (node.val === target) return node;
    node = target < node.val ? node.left : node.right;
  }
  return null;
}
```

The catch: O(h), not O(log n). Insert sorted data into a naive BST and it degenerates into a linked list with O(n) lookup. That's precisely why self-balancing trees exist. Mentioning this is a strong follow-up answer.

## Trigger phrases

**"tree"**, **"binary tree"**, **"BST"**, **"level order"**, **"depth"**, **"ancestor"**, **"path from root"**, **"nested"**, **"hierarchy"**, **"DOM traversal"**.

## Trade-offs and follow-ups

**"Iterative instead of recursive?"** — Use an explicit stack for DFS. You'd do this when the tree could be deep enough to overflow the call stack. JS caps around 10k frames.

**"DFS or BFS?"** — BFS for shortest path and anything level-based, but it holds a whole level in memory — O(w) where w is the widest level. DFS is O(h) space. On a wide shallow tree, DFS wins on memory; on a deep narrow one, BFS does.

**"Validate a BST."** — The trap: checking only `left < node < right` locally is wrong, because a node deep in the left subtree can still exceed the root. You must pass down a `(min, max)` range. This is a very common gotcha — knowing it in advance is worth a lot.

