Strings in JavaScript are **immutable sequences of UTF-16 code units**. Every "modification" allocates a new string. That single fact drives most string-problem strategy.

To put it simply: you can't edit a string in place, so when a problem needs mutation you convert to an array, do the work, and join back.

## The Problem immutability creates

Building a string in a loop looks harmless and isn't:

```javascript
let out = '';
for (const ch of str) out += ch;   // allocates a new string each time
```

Conceptually that's **O(n²)** — each concatenation copies everything so far. (Modern engines optimize this case with ropes, so it's rarely fatal in practice, but the *pattern* an interviewer wants to see is push-then-join.)

```javascript
const parts = [];
for (const ch of str) parts.push(ch);
const out = parts.join('');    // one allocation
```

Same reasoning applies to in-place reversal or swapping: `str[0] = 'x'` silently does nothing. Convert first:

```javascript
const chars = str.split('');   // now mutable
chars[0] = 'x';
const result = chars.join('');
```

## The three patterns that cover most string questions

### 1. Two pointers — palindromes and reversal

```javascript
function isPalindrome(s) {
  // Normalize first — the question almost always means alphanumeric only
  const clean = s.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '');
  let left = 0;
  let right = clean.length - 1;

  while (left < right) {
    if (clean[left] !== clean[right]) return false;
    left++;
    right--;
  }

  return true;
}
```

Ask before you code: *"Should I ignore case and punctuation?"* The answer is almost always yes, and asking is itself part of the evaluation.

### 2. Frequency map — anagrams and character counts

```javascript
function isAnagram(a, b) {
  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;   // cheap early exit

  const counts = new Map();
  for (const ch of a) counts.set(ch, (counts.get(ch) ?? 0) + 1);

  for (const ch of b) {
    const n = counts.get(ch);
    if (!n) return false;      // absent or already exhausted
    counts.set(ch, n - 1);
  }

  return true;
}
```

**O(n)**. The obvious alternative — sorting both and comparing — is O(n log n) but one line: `a.split('').sort().join('') === b.split('').sort().join('')`. Write the fast one, *mention* the short one. Showing you know both and chose deliberately is the point.

### 3. Sliding window — substrings

Any "longest/shortest substring such that…" question. Covered in [/algorithm/4](/algorithm/4).

## What actually is a JavaScript string?

A sequence of **UTF-16 code units**, not characters. Most of the time that distinction is invisible. It stops being invisible with emoji and non-BMP characters, which take **two** code units:

```javascript
'👋'.length;              // 2 — not 1
'👋'.split('').reverse(); // broken — splits the surrogate pair
[...'👋'].length;         // 1 — spread iterates by code point
```

So `[...str]` and `for...of` iterate by **code point**, while `str[i]`, `.split('')`, and `.length` work on **code units**.

You will almost certainly not need this. But saying *"I'm using `split('')` here — worth noting it breaks on emoji, and `[...str]` would be safer if the input can contain them"* is a genuinely senior observation that costs one sentence.

## The methods worth knowing cold

| Method | Note |
| --- | --- |
| `slice(start, end)` | Handles negatives: `s.slice(-3)` is the last three |
| `substring(a, b)` | Swaps arguments if out of order, clamps negatives to 0 — prefer `slice` |
| `split(sep)` | `split('')` → code units; `split(' ')` on a double space yields an empty string |
| `at(-1)` | Last character, cleanly |
| `padStart` / `padEnd` | Time and date formatting |
| `replaceAll` | No regex needed; `replace` only hits the first match without a `/g` flag |
| `localeCompare` | Correct string sorting; plain `<` is code-unit order |
| `trim` / `trimStart` / `trimEnd` | Always normalize user input |

The default `sort()` gotcha is a favorite: it converts to strings, so `[10, 9, 1].sort()` gives `[1, 10, 9]`. Always pass a comparator for numbers.

## Trigger phrases

**"palindrome"**, **"anagram"**, **"substring"**, **"reverse the words"**, **"first unique character"**, **"valid parentheses"** (that's a stack — [/algorithm/6](/algorithm/6)), **"compress"**, **"longest common prefix"**.

## Trade-offs and follow-ups

**"Can you do it without extra space?"** — Two pointers on a character array is O(1) extra. A frequency map is O(k) where k is the alphabet size — often described as O(1) if the alphabet is fixed at 26 or 128. Saying "O(1) because the alphabet is bounded" is a nice precise answer.

**"What about Unicode?"** — Covered above. One sentence, then move on.

**"Reverse the words in a sentence, not the characters."** — `s.trim().split(/\s+/).reverse().join(' ')`. The `\s+` regex rather than `' '` is what handles repeated spaces — that's the actual test.

